course: dictionary
lesson: 5353
title: Kin, family and related
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Kin is a word for your family and relatives, the people you are related to. In «of Cossack kin», the words around kin show it means related to the Cossacks, the way family members are related to each other.
explain: Kin is almost always plural. You say «my kin», not «a kin». Look for the words around it: in «next of kin», the word next shows who is closest.
explain: Old-fashioned books may use kin for people or animals of the same kind. Kith and kin is a phrase for friends and family together, so kith (friends) and kin (family) go side by side.
fact: Kin means your family and relatives.
fact: Kin is plural only: you say «my kin», not «a kin».
fact: «Next of kin» means your closest relative, often named on forms for emergencies.
fact: «Kith and kin» means friends and family.
fact: Old-fashioned: kin can mean people or animals of the same kind.
exercise: What does kin mean? | A large river | A shop that sells food | *Your family and relatives | A kind of hat
exercise: On an emergency form, «next of kin» means | A stranger who wrote the form | *Your closest relative | Your best friend at school
exercise: In the phrase «kith and kin», which word means family and relatives? | kith | and | *kin
test: Why do we say «my kin» and never «a kin»? | Kin is the name for one person | Kin means a single cat | *Kin is plural only | Kin only means friends
test: The anthem calls Ukrainians «of Cossack kin». What does kin mean here? | *Related, as family is related | A dance | A shop | A pet
exam: In an old book, «kin» might be used to mean | Only family | Only people, never animals | Only friends | *People or animals of the same kind
